/* ---------------- findings ---------------- */
function viewFindings(m){
  const li = (h, b) => `<li><b>${h}</b> ${b}</li>`;
  m.innerHTML = card('What the AVB1 is up to (beyond the manual)', `<ol class="small">
    ${li('Hold ON/OFF: done in software, not hardware.', 'The ON/OFF contact latches REL1, so the PICs boot at once. U200 then counts hold_num 20 × 256 main-loop passes (on_off_hold) before accepting the press. On battery, an early release makes proccontrol send tx1,0 = OFF, and U4 (translate_data → relay_reset) pulses REL1’s reset coil through RB2/Q8. In the run the hold takes about 1.2–1.6 s, depending on how long a pass is.')}
    ${li('A two-second meter blackout after every audio change.', 'Each audio button runs BCF PORTD,4 (CAL), and meter_led_on lights the meters again only after lock_num1 25 × 256 locked passes, about 2 s (the constant was halved from 50). The manual does not mention it.')}
    ${li('The comments describe a protocol the code no longer uses.', 'uart.asm in both U200 and U4 documents tx0 as “a1,a0 … 10 = sdi mode, term, hp” and tx1 as an audio level (0–90 / 0–60). The code sends a1:a0 = 11 for SDI and power flags in tx1. U4 translate_data treats 10 and 11 the same. The U200 header says it talks to the main board “using the SPI”, but the link is a 9600 Bd UART. U200 init calls RC6/RC7 “RS485”, although the only DS485 on the board (U7) is the AES line receiver.')}
    ${li('Button nets are mirrored.', 'In avb1-2iss3.net the U200 pin nets are named PB1…PB12, but each one goes to the opposite switch in its row: net “PB6” goes to switch PB1 (7/8), net “PB1” to PB6 (VIDEO ANALOG), net “PB7” to PB12 (SDI), and so on. The firmware follows the switch refdes, so it works. The port comments in U200 init.asm mix the two schemes (for example “RA1 PB11 (AES AUDIO)”, but RA1 is the 13/14 button).')}
    ${li('Stale or wrong comments on constants.', 'adconv~3.asm says “less than 9.60 volts” for BATTERY_INSTALLED (113 = 9.00 V), “12.25 V” for LOW_BATTERY_VIDEO (150 = 12.00 V) and “11.94 V” for LOW_BATTERY_AUDIO (149 = 11.75 V). In U201 the T2CON comment says “POSTSCALE 1:7, PRESCALE 1:8”, but 0x45 is 1:9 and 1:4. The interrupt routine clears TMR1IE where its comment says Timer2 (harmless, because PEIE is cleared next).')}
    ${li('U200 init reads the wrong pin.', 'init.asm: “movlw lock_num2 · btfsc portb,3 ;if AC=1 … movlw lock_num4”. AC_PRES is on RB2; RB3 is the 11/12 button, so the A/D start-up delay depends on that button and not on AC.')}
    ${li('Charging is not as “smart” as the manual says.', 'Manual: charging begins “only if the battery is sufficiently discharged i.e. when the low battery indicator is active”. The code charges whenever the first reading after the cord goes in is above 9.0 V (battery present) and below BATTERY_MIN 14.0 V, then follows the voltage peak (BATTERY_REF, minus 3 counts) to decide when it is full. While FAST charge is on, the ON/OFF lamp is lit (yes_charge: BSF led2,4).')}
    ${li('Changing power source resets U200 on purpose.', 'previous_battery_mode sets ac,1 and press_flag,2. eewrite saves everything, then runs “timeout: goto timeout ;let timeout occur for the system to reset”. That needs the watchdog, but no __CONFIG line is in any source, and the .pjt files say WDTEnable=0 (emulator setting). The fuses must have been set by hand in the programmer ' + inf + '.')}
    ${li('Every button press writes the EEPROM six times.', 'eewrite stores output1, output2, audio_mode, video_mode, ac and the 0xAA marker (address 30) on each press, so about 24 ms of EEPROM writes. With the 16F747 (v1.05) the recall was commented out, and its header says so: “when changing power sources no recall of input”.')}
    ${li('The PK LED is about −4 dBFS, not +3.', 'U201 lights LD16 and holds it for 1.5 s when the half-scale A/D copy (R253/R254) reads ≥ 190, which is 7.6 V at the peak detector. The bar’s “0 dB” is about 1.22 V (table rows 61–68), so the PK LED trips about 15.8 dB over reference. With 0 VU = −20 dBFS that is about −4 dBFS, close to the manual’s “Peak Level −3 dB below Full Scale”. The comments still say “+3”, a leftover from the AVM4 meter this code came from ' + inf + '.')}
    ${li('Dead code in the meter and main board.', 'U201 TABLE3 (dot display) is never reached: PK_LEFT/PK_RIGHT set TIMER_FLAG,4, and the “goto peak_data” was commented out in favour of vu_data. U4 run_stop has two RETURNs before the CS8415 stop/restart sequence, so that sequence never runs. U4 RDBYTE (I²C read) is never called.')}
    ${li('Label names from another meter scale.', 'TABLE2 labels say signal10, signal7, signal5, signal3, signal1, but the rows they serve are −16, −8, −4, −2 and −1 dB on the AVB1 scale. They are probably AVM4 names.')}
    ${li('Unfitted and unused hardware.', 'The GS7005 LOCK output (LOCK7005) only reaches the ICSP header pin H1-2 and RB7 through R46, which is DNI; the firmware has “btfsc portb,7 ;test for carrier detect” commented out. SDI_SIGNAL_CHECK’s comment names a “CLC016”; the BOM has a CLC014. The ADV7171 gets only VD9…VD2 (8 of 10 bits).')}
    ${li('Shared CS8415/GS9023 lines.', 'RA0/RA1 are both CS8415 CDIN/CCLK and GS9023 A0/A1. With the GS9023 unpowered the CS8415 control writes were corrupted (v1.01 header: “voltage divider”). The v1.01 fix powers SDI whenever AES is chosen; v1.02 says “The initial problem was not fixed.” In v1.04 check_aes_audio still powers SDI for AES. The model sends 0 on CDIN whenever SDI is off ' + inf + '.')}
    ${li('ADV7175 and back.', 'v1.03 (June 2003) changed the I²C table for an ADV7175 (and the #working folder of 31 March 2005 still has it). v1.04 (30 March 2005) restores the ADV7171 values, and the 2006 ISSUE 3 BOM lists an ADV7171.')}
    ${li('Project files point elsewhere.', 'Every .pjt says Path=M:\\AmS\\PROGRAM\\ and source 0=ams1v101.asm, so they were cloned from the AMS1 project. v104/avb.mcp opens avb1-1v103.asm. U201 #INCLUDEs “bar_me~1.ASM” and “ADCONV~1.ASM” (8.3 short names of bar_meter.asm and ADCONVERTER.ASM), so it only builds on the original FAT/short-name drive. The .err files for v1.04/1.05 show Error[173] “source file path exceeds 62 characters” on H:\\ENGINEERING\\WBSPRODUCTS\\…, and u200v105.err a 16F874/16F747 processor mismatch. All three need /c- and /rdec (case-insensitive, decimal), as the project options say.')}
    ${li('U201 has no crystal.', 'It is clocked from U200’s OSC2 through R242 (net OSCOUT), so both front PICs share X201.')}
    ${li('Artwork '+Y(2026)+': already CorelDRAW.', `The panels were built from the CorelDRAW silks in the first place: ${SIM.src(AVB_SILK+'avb1-frontssv1.cdr','avb1-frontssv1.cdr')}, ${SIM.src(AVB_SILK+'avb1-sidessv2curve.cdr','avb1-sidessv2curve.cdr')} and ${SIM.src(AVB_SILK+'avb1-REARssv3curve.cdr','avb1-REARssv3curve.cdr')}, with the ink layers cut from them. No further artwork turned up: the other AVB CDRs are earlier issues (REARssv1/ssv2, sidessv1), Backup_of_ copies, the manual page ${SIM.src(AVB_DIR+'AVB1Manual Page6.cdr','AVB1Manual Page6.cdr')} with its control legends, and ${SIM.src(AVB_DIR+'AVBControls.pdf','AVBControls.pdf')}, a CorelDRAW 10 print of the meter and control legends. The front exists only as a live-text issue 1. There is no “curve” front file, unlike the side and the rear.`)}
    ${li('The meter scale skips −12.', `${SIM.src(AVB_DIR+'AVBControls.pdf','AVBControls.pdf')} prints the bar legend as −30 −28 −24 −20 −16 −8 −4 −2 −1 R +1 +2 +3 +6 +9 +12. The step from −16 to −8 is the only 8 dB gap, so there is no −12 mark, and the reference is the letter “R”, not 0.`)}
    ${li('Rear label versions.', 'avb1-REARssv2 (Oct 2003): “110-220 VAC 50-60 Hz, 1 A.S.B.”; REARssv3 (Nov 2003): “120mA CHARGING / 160mA CHARGING IN USE” and a typo “450mA MAXC”; REARssv3temp/curve (Jan 2004): “60mA CHARGING MIN / 160mA CHARGING MAX”, internal DC +24 V 300 mA, battery BBS2085 +12 V NiCd 450 mA max. The manual says 90–230 V.')}
    ${li('Battery size, compared with RD69.', 'The label’s 450 mA maximum with the manual’s 2.0 h minimum (SDI + embedded) suggests about 0.9 Ah, about 11 Wh, usable. The RD69 plugin (rd69-vidbuddy-madibuddy.js) assumed a 21.6 Wh pack for the same hours; on this evidence the AVB1 pack was about half that ' + inf + '.')}
    ${li('Front panel changes.', 'AVB1-M3 rev 2: “MOUNTING HOLES AND CUTOUT ARE CHANGED FOR NEW DISPLAY MONITOR”; rev 3: “.352 SQUARE HOLE WAS .340” (13 button holes). After painting, the panel goes back to the metal shop for paint removal (AVB1-M3-PRP; grounding areas ' + inf + ').')}
    ${li('Not Audio Video Bridging.', 'The folder name “AVB VID BUDDY” is the product code AVB1 (“Audio/Video Buddy”). RD63/RD79 are about IEEE AVB networking; RD69’s 2013 “Vid Buddy” is a later idea based on this unit.')}
  </ol>`) + card('Model assumptions', `<ul class="small">
    <li>The firmware is not rewritten: the plugin carries the archived sources (U200 v1.04, U201 v1.00 and AVB1-1 v1.04, the 16F874 versions that match the ${Y(2006)} BOM), assembles them with an MPASM-compatible assembler and runs them on three PIC16F874 cores. The tasks were split this way because the BOM lists three 16F874s.</li>
    <li>The parts around them are behavioural models: CS8415 (register 2 source, register 4 RUN, register 16 UNLOCK; lock time ${LOCK_S*1e3} ms ${inf}), GS9023 (register 0 bits 5 and 3 as the firmware reads them; group from register 3), ADV7171 (I²C slave at 0x54 that acknowledges), relays, and the peak detectors (gain, 1 ms / 201 ms from the netlist; 0 VU ≈ 1.225 V from the TABLE rows ${inf}).</li>
    <li>Fuses: watchdog on, power-up timer on, HS oscillator ${inf}. The watchdog period is the nominal 18 ms × 128.</li>
    <li>Panel colour: AVB1-M2/M3/M4 “EXTRA FINE TEXTURED WBS BLACK”. Ink colours: no photograph of the AVB1 is in the archive, so the legends are white and the graphic layer is green, after the sister ABB-1 ${inf}. Legends are re-set in SVG at the CorelDRAW positions, because libcdr loses the text alignment.</li>
    <li>REL1/REL2/REL3 contact roles come from the netlist and the U4 comments (“disconnect battery” / “connect battery”) ${inf}. The unit runs whenever the AC rail is up or REL1 is latched.</li></ul>`) +
  card('Sources', `<ul class="small mono"><li>AVB - #Software/…AvbU200 #v104/u200v104.asm, init.asm, mode.asm, uart.asm, adconv~3.asm, ee_data.asm, U200v104.pjt, v104.mcp, u200v104.err; #v101–#v105, #new, #new iss1 (diffs)</li><li>…AvbU201 #V100/u201v100.asm, INIT.ASM, bar_meter.asm, ADCONVERTER.ASM, U201v100.pjt; #V101</li><li>…Avb1-1 #v104/avb1-1v104.asm, init.asm, uart.asm, b1v104.pjt, avb.mcp; #v100…#V105, #v10xB (PAL), #working</li><li>AVB - #PCB/avb1-2iss3.net, AVB1-2ISS3.bom, AVB1-2ISS3.pnp, avb1-2iss3.ECO (P-CAD 2002, 2006|39)</li><li>AVB - #SILK/avb1-frontssv1.cdr (Inkscape → SVG → assets/avb-vid-buddy-front-ink.png), avb1-sidessv2curve.cdr (→ avb-vid-buddy-side-ink.png), avb1-REARssv3curve.cdr (→ avb-vid-buddy-rear-ink.png), REARssv2/ssv3/ssv3temp</li><li>AVBManual.doc (2002|35), AVB1Manual Page6.cdr, AVB1MANU.cdr, AVBControls.pdf (2004|37)</li><li>AVB - #MECHANICAL/AVB1-M1…M12, BATTERY-PACK, CARRYING-HANDLE</li><li>siumulation-plugins/abb1.js, rd69-vidbuddy-madibuddy.js, rd79-avb-monitor.js</li></ul>`);
}

